            "text": "{\n  \"study_topic_id\": \"study-22e9cdea36934c5fd18a\",\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"text\": \"Ohm’s law relates the voltage drop across an electrical component, the current through it, and its resistance: V = IR. It is useful for calculating any one of these quantities when the other two are known, helping describe and analyze simple circuits. The relationship reflects experiments by German physicist Georg Simon Ohm in the 19th century. It is an empirical rule, not a pattern every material or component follows: when a component’s current is proportional to voltage over a range of conditions, it is called ohmic. For an ohmic component, doubling voltage while resistance stays constant doubles current.\",\n    \"source_urls\": [\n      \"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/19-1-ohms-law\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"key_facts\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"In V = IR, V is voltage, I is current, and R is resistance; the same relationship can be rearranged to find any one of them.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/19-1-ohms-law\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Resistance is measured in ohms (Ω); one ohm equals one volt per ampere (1 Ω = 1 V/A).\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/19-1-ohms-law\",\n        \"https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/sp330.pdf\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Ohm’s law describes an experimentally observed relationship; it does not apply to every material or component in all conditions.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/19-1-ohms-law\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"A material or component that follows the proportional voltage-current relationship is called ohmic.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/19-1-ohms-law\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Georg Simon Ohm, a German physicist, experimentally established the relationship in the 19th century.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/19-1-ohms-law\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"sources\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"19.1 Ohm's law - Physics\",\n      \"url\": \"https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/19-1-ohms-law\",\n      \"publisher\": \"OpenStax\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24\",\n      \"supports\": \"Equation and variable meanings, Ohm’s experimental work, the empirical nature and limits of the relationship, ohmic materials, and the ohm as V/A.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"The International System of Units (SI), NIST Special Publication 330, 2001 Edition\",\n      \"url\": \"https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/sp330.pdf\",\n      \"publisher\": \"National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), U.S. Department of Commerce\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24\",\n      \"supports\": \"SI definitions relating the volt, ampere, and ohm.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"research_queries\": [\n    \"site:openstax.org Ohm's law voltage current resistance\",\n    \"site:physics.nist.gov ohm unit volt ampere SI ohm\",\n    \"site:phys.libretexts.org Ohm's law empirical relationship current voltage resistance\",\n    \"site:phet.colorado.edu Ohm's law voltage current resistance simulation\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\",\n  \"unresolved_concerns\": [],\n  \"self_check_complete\": true\n}"
